{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"07a522045fcebbe30559d89d169c848e59b265f9db02484374662ac1f2a8c995","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:29+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:4c52b8a1c591a794fe33211b","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:interdisciplinary-science:01M0HJR2FRA0MQW35V99Z36KZF","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The theorem is a direct consequence of completeness: if an NP'_R-complete problem lies in P'_R, then every NP'_R problem reduces to it and can be solved in P'_R, forcing class equality. The meta-principle that reductions transfer difficulty and completeness organizes classes supplies the general mechanism.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:29+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.75,"content_digest":"a3ef43c4ecfa26c1a24d9ddca19b139663821118377aee7f9ca04596eafdd841","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:48:05+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"d1f86aae08b97709d53464a0e9e01bf43ae9ec6b9238bb114fe53bac9509a03a","input_tokens":5771,"latency_ms":29806,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"7dc36eec4c9e7f39c261ef17a633e32440712f65bbfb36f5956586680f941bdd","output_tokens":2550,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:5d0352d96c2da9d6866f37ef","meta_principle_id":"meta:mathematics-logic:invariance","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:physics:01M0NCSEGEPRFZ76M41SZSN0D7","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle identifies a quantity—total relative area between shape and image—that remains unchanged under resolution lowering for full shapes, directly illustrating an invariant under a declared transformation. The thin-stroke case shows the same quantity is not invariant for all shape classes, matching the meta-principle's condition that invariants are meaningful only relative to a transformation class and object class.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:48:05+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.55,"content_digest":"280368a63746653f54667d3d00eebc1b954115996993e29f4417fa15701c55be","created_at":"2026-08-22T15:28:16+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"500989856546bdae95b034ed7fa30d8b042baca134628e6541dc64ecd5ca004a","input_tokens":5923,"latency_ms":40979,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"b3d7ea5d987a97ae2222425dfb9702434d585fbc50b3e7c9ae848ef16888a6da","output_tokens":2728,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:8072af1c4eab17652acada14","meta_principle_id":"meta:statistics-causality:sufficiency","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0N1B7TZ0G0VZSG50WHHBHSG","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The sufficiency meta-principle states that a statistic can preserve information about a parameter only within a specified model; here, neither EEG nor ECG alone is sufficient for the brain-state classification task because the other modality contributes orthogonal information. The new claim is consistent with this information-theoretic framing, though sufficiency is model-relative and the empirical claim does not depend on it for validity.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T15:28:16+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.6,"content_digest":"da60fd2fddfd8e926f00842788c69588ee1cb4dd07443d623ee999b07caeb873","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:37:50+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"f49fe2f1a8ccccf756873a6183e1c0a5c81a7a1037a4dda89bb5c3ce10e46c36","input_tokens":5877,"latency_ms":34997,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"c3db54b24b98abc889c9947757133b6dea5bf007e89df54272ef2fef9e2dcd61","output_tokens":2487,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:a55042dadd9978b7fa5f12b0","meta_principle_id":"meta:mathematics-logic:fixed-point","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:scientific-discovery:01M0QDDYKT605M7JZZPCDPQ1SR","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Existence of equilibria in finite games with mixed strategies is typically established through fixed-point arguments on compact convex strategy spaces with continuous or upper hemicontinuous best-response correspondences. The minimax-regret equilibrium existence proof is naturally grounded in this theorem family, though the source does not explicitly name the fixed-point theorem.","relation_type":"depends_on","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T13:37:50+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
